    constexpr Fenrir::TypeDescription Fenrir_Vehicle{
        .name = "Fenrir::Vehicle",
        .package = "FenrirPhysics",
        .role = Fenrir::Role::Component,
        .size = sizeof(Fenrir::Vehicle),
        .alignment = alignof(Fenrir::Vehicle),
        .construct = &Fenrir::ConstructValue<Fenrir::Vehicle>,
        .destroy = &Fenrir::DestroyValue<Fenrir::Vehicle>,
        .doc = "Makes the entity's dynamic RigidBody a vehicle (ADR-0015): the body is its chassis, the only part of the vehicle that collides. Its wheels are VehicleWheel components on wheel entities of their own, each naming this entity. Chassis-local +Y is up and -Z is forward, by convention: turn a model facing another way on a child.\n\nGame code drives it by writing `input` in `Tick`. With every default, a wheeled vehicle drives every wheel through an automatic five-speed gearbox.\n\nPlain data that physics compares each tick with what it last applied (ADR-0011). A vehicle with no wheels is valid but inert. Its body needs a collider it can make, as any body does; the chassis must not be scaled to size its look: give it an unscaled BoxShape of the right size and a scaled look as a child.\n\nWithout a dynamic RigidBody with a solid collider, or beside a CharacterBody, it is inactive, with one warning each time it becomes so: a chassis turned kinematic or static, or losing its RigidBody or collider, is a plain body (if any), its `state` and its wheels' reset and its wheel entities left where they were. Valid again, it is built fresh with nothing carried over, so a car made kinematic for a cutscene restarts at idle in neutral.\n\nRespawning is a Transform write: a teleport keeps the engine speed and wheel spin, as a body's velocity is kept, and the wheels are posed on the chassis's new pose at once. A rebuild (a wheel added, removed or relinked, or the chassis's body rebuilt in one tick, as when its collider is swapped) keeps the engine speed, the gear and each remaining wheel's spin; a new wheel starts still. A change to the chassis's scale moves the wheels with it, with no rebuild.\n\nWhat is ground is decided by layers alone: each wheel's probe filters as the chassis's CollisionFilter does, finding only what the chassis would collide with, and never a trigger, the chassis itself or a character. Probes ignore joints, as queries do, so a joint's `collideConnected` never hides ground. A character is never ground: a vehicle meets one with its chassis, as an immovable wall, and to roll over something make it a dynamic or static body. A character pushes the chassis with its CharacterBody::pushForce, as it pushes any body, which barely moves a car, and one standing on the chassis rides it and presses on it with its weight. A wheel on a dynamic body pushes, loads and wakes it; on moving ground its slip is measured against the ground's velocity, so a vehicle parked on a kinematic platform rides it through tyre grip. Over light dynamic bodies the chassis dips: an artifact of the solver's mass ratio.\n\nOnly the chassis collides: queries and contact events see its body and name its entity, a body falling onto a wheel passes through it to the chassis, and QueryFilter::ignoreBody takes the chassis entity to skip the vehicle.\n\nParking is the handbrake: a vehicle at rest falls asleep as any body does, but never while `forward` is held or its engine is above idle. Asleep, it keeps its last `state`, its wheels' spin 0. Any change to `input`, any edit to a vehicle component (Vehicle, VehicleWheel, VehicleDifferential, VehicleAntiRollBar) and any rebuild wake the chassis, so pressing the throttle always drives. Stated cost: registering any vehicle stops the physics backend skipping its update of a world where everything sleeps, so a parked vehicle costs a little every tick.\n\nTowing is an ordinary Joint: a trailer is any dynamic body, usually a TrailerVehicle, joined to the chassis, usually by a `ball` at the hitch. With the default `collideConnected = false` the hitch does not fight the chassis, and the joint links the two so they sleep and wake together. A wheeled vehicle given no input also tows, staying in neutral. Trailer brakes are game code's: copy the tractor's `brake` and `handbrake` into the trailer's `input` each tick; nothing links them. Stated limit: a trailer much heavier than its tractor jitters at the hitch.\n\nGetting in and out is game code's: there is no seat component. To get in, set the character's CharacterBody::enabled to false, which hides it from bodies, queries, triggers and the wheels' probes, then parent its entity under the chassis (or a seat child of it) at a seat-local Transform. A disabled character is never posed by physics and never part of the chassis's compound (ADR-0014), so it rides along at the chassis's interpolated pose. To get out, unparent it and write its world pose beside the door, clear it of the chassis and anything else with PhysicsWorld::Depenetrate (or move it there with MoveCast), then enable it again.\n\nFlipped and stuck vehicles are game code's too: there is no helper. Notice `state.wheelsOnGround == 0` with the chassis's up turned well away from world up for a while, then right it with a Transform write (upright, a little higher, its velocities zeroed) or a torque. A `maxTilt` of about 60\302\260 stops rollovers altogether.",
        .fields = Fenrir_Vehicle_Fields,
    };

